###2 Part 2: Take-Home (Spatial Lag Model and Model Comparison)

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

##2.1 Spatial Lag Model (SLM)

#a)
slm_fit = lagsarlm(lifeExp ~ log_gdp,
                   data = world,
                   listw = listw,
                   zero.policy = TRUE)

summary(slm_fit)

#The estimated ρˆ (rho) parameter is -0.0043 and is not statistically significant (p = 0.806).
#The coefficient on log gdp 5.55 and highly statistically significant (p < 0.001)

#b)
#The parameter ρ captures the extent to which a country’s life expectancy depends 
#on the life expectancy of neighboring countries. In this case, ρ is very close to
#zero and not statistically significant, suggesting that there is no meaningful 
#spatial diffusion in life expectancy.

#c)
#The coefficient on log GDP per capita in the SLM is not the marginal effect because 
#changes in GDP affect not only a country’s own life expectancy but also that of
#neighboring countries through spatial feedback.

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

##2.2 Direct and Indirect Effects

#a)
set.seed(123)

imp = impacts(slm_fit, listw = listw, R = 500)
summary(imp)

#The direct effect of log GDP per capita is 5.55, the indirect effect is -0.02,
#and the total effect is 5.52. The direct effect is almost identical to the coefficient
#on log GDP per capita from the SLM output and very similar to the OLS estimate.

#b)
#The indirect effect shows how a change in GDP per capita in one country affects 
#life expectancy in other countries through spatial spillovers. 
#In this case, the indirect effect is very small and statistically insignificant
#meaning that increases in GDP in one country do not really affect life expectancy
#in neighboring countries.

#c)
#The total effect is typically larger than the direct effect in the SLM because 
#changes in one unit propagate through neighboring units and feed back into the 
#original unit via the spatial multiplier.

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

##2.3 Model Comparison

#a)
#The AIC for the OLS model is 965.99, for the SLM it is 967.93.
#SEM has the lowest AIC, indicating the best model fit after accounting for model complexity.

#b)
#

